{
  "stage": "write",
  "item": "ch01.s05",
  "inputs_hash": "9ca383a970376bfc88cce7ec8e68709a57939e88fd09aa53a172f4286eccd0b0",
  "output_hash": "0c5deb1c0bd3aaa4474aae9acd1e3fe02425a056da99221dcb2a9bf662ce7218",
  "output": {
    "section_id": "ch01.s05",
    "paragraphs": [
      {
        "id": "ch01.s05.p01",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch01.s05.p01.01",
            "text": "Why does the distinction between open-source and closed-source models matter for learning large language models now?",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch01.s05.p01.02",
            "text": "An open-source model is one whose architecture is available to the public, so anyone can see the architecture.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@405",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@407",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@409",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@412",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@414"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch01.s05.p01.03",
            "text": "By contrast, models released by OpenAI are described here as usually closed source.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@417"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch01.s05.p01.04",
            "text": "A closed-source model is one for which the weights, the architecture, and many other details about the model itself are not released publicly.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@420",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@422",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@425"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch01.s05.p02",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch01.s05.p02.01",
            "text": "This distinction matters because access affects what can be inspected and studied directly.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch01.s05.p02.02",
            "text": "The comparison described for 2022 says that, when the field of large language models was booming, most things were pretty closed source.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@430",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@433",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@435",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@437"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch01.s05.p02.03",
            "text": "GPT-4 is presented as a closed-source model released in 2023.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@437",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@439",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@448"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch01.s05.p02.04",
            "text": "In the 2024 comparison, the gap between open-source and closed-source models is described as slowly decreasing.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@451",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@454",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@456"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch01.s05.p02.05",
            "text": "When Llama 3.1 is released in that comparison, it is shown as performing at the same level as GPT-4.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@456",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@459",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@461"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch01.s05.p02.06",
            "text": "The point drawn from this comparison is that the information needed to learn is available right now through open-source models.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@465",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@467",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@469"
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "id": "ch01.s05.p03",
        "sentences": [
          {
            "id": "ch01.s05.p03.01",
            "text": "That makes the learning path less dependent on access to closed systems.",
            "type": "C",
            "cites": []
          },
          {
            "id": "ch01.s05.p03.02",
            "text": "A strong reference for learning large language models is Sebastian Raschka’s book, which is described as one of the best books on the topic.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@741",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@743",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@746",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@747"
            ]
          },
          {
            "id": "ch01.s05.p03.03",
            "text": "That book is also used as the reference material for this course.",
            "type": "F",
            "cites": [
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@750",
              "Xpr8D6LeAtw@752"
            ]
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
  }
}
